  {
    "episode_id": 46645,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/6/46645.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, as I've said repeatedly, if you were to design investigation that was specifically wanted to create suspicion and drive conspiracy theories, do you go about it exactly the way the FBI, Secret Service and Department of Justice are going about this? Almost completely opaque. The only time they provide any information is when there's ramping up a public pressure. Yeah, I think our congressional investigation will end up being an investigation of the investigation. It was my staff. Again, what's unusual about my staff is I'm the ranking member on the permanent subcommittee investigation. This should be the premier investigatory body of the Senate. But you can tell the Senate is not particularly serious about investigations. I have five staff members. That's the budget they give me. I've got five staff members, but they're experienced. So almost immediately after the assassination, 10th on July 13th, my investigators started contacting a local law enforcement. And I would say to this day, What we know about what happened in January 13th or July 13th is coming primarily from local law enforcement of videos, audios, I went to the council from rally goers, not from the feds. So, I mean, that's why, you know, We have those very early conversations and I issued my preliminary findings within a week where we laid out initial timeline. We continue to update that. By the way, that's the way you do investigations. You do a timeline and you keep filling in the blanks. You keep looking for information. And what you know, that is investor reporting and it's unbelievably revealing. So anyway, so we provided that timeline. We continue to update it for the public and really trying to solicit more information. So if somebody knows something and they look at my timeline, they're missing this piece, they can call into our office. So that's how we're going about doing this. But the feds aren't really providing us anything. FBI is interviewed over a thousand individuals. In a hearing a couple of weeks after the assassination attempt, I asked Deputy Director of the FBI abate, you know, we want your the transcriptions of those interviews, you know, the 302s. And he said he's going to check on and get him to us soon. Well, we have not got one 302 yet. We've not got one of their transcriptions. We've been able to interview less than 12 people. And, of course, Acting Director Rowe said that those interviews would start within days that week, a little bit more than a week. But it's not exactly like we're getting just a flood of people to interview, plus they're withholding documents. When they give us documents, for example, documents pertaining to particular person we're interviewing, but we'll get him sometimes just minutes before the interview and with heavy reaction. So you can't make an effective use of the documentation. So this is my first rodeo with the feds on this. This is exactly how they thank cooperation. They spoon-feed you just enough to keep the subpoenas at bay. Now, again, if I were chairman, I'd be issuing subpoenas. But Democrats generally are not real fond of investigating government. And so, you know, Gary Peters and Roots of Blumenthal, they're not issuing subpoenas. Again, we have been in session. We'll be back in next week. I hope to have a meeting and put more pressure on the process because the American people have right to know these things.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_02",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
